{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Outer Sports Ballers — Architecture Onboarding",
  "description": "A top-to-bottom walkthrough for new engineers: BFF auth (HttpOnly cookies + Zod), RSC route groups, the data proxy, and the Three.js gameplay loop.",
  "ref": "develop",
  "steps": [
    {
      "directory": "src",
      "title": "Source Root",
      "description": "Everything lives under `src/`. The shape: `app/` for App Router routes (with route groups `(auth)`, `(main)`, `(gameplay)` — each enforces its own auth posture), `components/` for UI grouped by feature, `hooks/` for data hooks and Three.js layer hooks, `lib/` for shared utilities, `types/` for domain types. Notice there is no `contexts/` and no `providers/` — auth is owned by the BFF route handlers under `app/api/auth/*` and a single TanStack Query hook (`hooks/useSession.ts`)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/middleware.ts",
      "line": 14,
      "title": "Edge Middleware — Cookie Guard",
      "description": "First line of defense before any page is served. It checks an `osb_at` cookie (HttpOnly, set by Next.js BFF routes) and falls back to the legacy `access_token` cookie name for one-deploy cutover compatibility. Public routes are narrow: `/login`, `/signup`, `/forgot-password`. Anything else without a cookie is redirected to `/login` (line 27). The matcher (line 36) excludes `api/`, `_next/static`, and asset paths so internal routes and the BFF proxies are never gated by middleware."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/lib/auth/schemas.ts",
      "line": 49,
      "title": "User Schema & Profile Normalizer",
      "description": "Zod schemas are the single source of truth for auth shapes. `LoginInput`/`SignupInput` drive both BFF input validation and `react-hook-form` resolvers in the login/signup pages. `UserSchema` (line 35) is the *normalized* user object exposed to all UI. `normalizeProfile()` is the gotcha: the backend returns inconsistent fields (`userId` | `id` | `_id`, `username` | `name`, `roles` | `role`, `avatar` | `image`) — this function picks the right one and coerces `id` to a string so downstream consumers don't have to handle `string | number`."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/lib/auth/session.ts",
      "line": 25,
      "title": "Server-Side getSession() — RSC Auth Source",
      "description": "Marked `import \"server-only\"` (line 1) so it can never accidentally ship to the browser. RSC layouts await this to enforce auth before render. It reads the `osb_at` HttpOnly cookie via `next/headers`, fetches `/auth/profile` from the backend with the bearer, runs the response through `normalizeProfile()`, and returns `User | null`. Wrapped in `react.cache` (line 25) so multiple `getSession()` calls inside one request hit the backend exactly once. The `getAccessToken()` helper (line 32) is the escape hatch for server actions that need to call the backend directly with the user's token."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/app/providers.tsx",
      "line": 33,
      "title": "Root Providers — QueryClient + Session Hydration",
      "description": "Mounted once in `src/app/layout.tsx` line 43 as `<Providers>`. Owns the singleton TanStack `QueryClient` (created via `useState` so each SSR request gets its own instance — see `makeQueryClient` line 17). `SessionHydrator` (line 38) is the bridge from RSC to client: route-group layouts pass the server-fetched `User | null` into it, which calls `qc.setQueryData(['session'], session)` once. That means the client `useSession()` hook returns hydrated data on first paint — no loading flash, no double-fetch."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/app/(main)/layout.tsx",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "Main Route Group — RSC Auth Gate",
      "description": "This is the auth boundary for every authed page (home, hangar, inventory, missions, store, profile, ranking). The layout is `async` and runs server-side: `await getSession()` then `redirect('/login')` if null (line 16). Below the gate, `<SessionHydrator session={session} />` seeds the client TanStack cache so the first client render has the session already. The `(gameplay)/layout.tsx` mirrors this exact pattern — both share the same auth posture without duplicating logic."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/app/api/auth/login/route.ts",
      "line": 36,
      "title": "BFF Auth Login — Backend Proxy + Cookie Setter",
      "description": "Representative example of the 6 BFF auth routes (login, signup, logout, me, guest, callback). Validates the request body with `LoginInputSchema` (line 28), proxies to the real backend at `${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/auth/login`, validates the response with `BackendAuthResponseSchema` (line 51), then sets the HttpOnly cookies (lines 68-71). Crucially, the JWT never touches client JS — it goes straight into the cookie header. The backend URL also never reaches the browser. Add new auth flows by mirroring this pattern: validate input → call backend → set/clear cookies → return user."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/app/api/backend/[...path]/route.ts",
      "line": 26,
      "title": "BFF Data Proxy — Token Injection",
      "description": "All non-auth backend traffic flows through this catch-all. It accepts any HTTP method (lines 92-96 export GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE all bound to `forward`), reads the `osb_at` HttpOnly cookie (line 40), strips browser cookies + hop-by-hop headers (line 45), and reissues the request to the backend with `Authorization: Bearer <token>` (line 49). On a 401 from the backend, it clears all auth cookies (lines 84-86) so the browser's next navigation is caught by middleware and redirected to `/login`. This is why no client code ever sees `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` in network tab — only `/api/backend/*`."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/lib/api/client.ts",
      "line": 98,
      "title": "Fetch Wrapper — Axios-Shaped, BFF-Routed",
      "description": "Replaces the deleted axios instance. Same call shape (`api.get/post/put/patch/delete` returning `{ data, status, headers }`) so the 13 data hooks didn't need per-call edits during the rewrite. Internally it rewrites every relative path through the `/api/backend` BFF prefix (`joinUrl`, line 22). Supports an axios-style `params` option for query strings. On 401, calls `handleUnauthorized()` (line 57) which navigates to `/login` (skipping if already there to avoid loops). When you write a new data hook, import `api` from `@/lib/api/client` and call `api.get('/missions/...')` — never call `fetch` directly."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/hooks/useSession.ts",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "useSession() — The Only Auth Hook",
      "description": "Replaces NextAuth's `useSession` AND the deleted `useAuth()` context. A single TanStack Query against `GET /api/auth/me`, keyed `['session']` (exported as `SESSION_QUERY_KEY` so mutations can update it). 5-minute staleTime, no refetch-on-window-focus (intentional — mid-game focus losses should not refetch the session). Returns `{ data: User | null, isLoading, ... }`. The hydration from `SessionHydrator` means `data` is populated synchronously on first render in `(main)` and `(gameplay)` route groups. Mutations (`useLogin`, `useSignup`, `useLogout`, `useGuestLogin`) live in `src/lib/auth/client.ts` and update the same query key on success."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/components/gameplay/ThreeGameplayCanvas.tsx",
      "line": 15,
      "title": "Gameplay Canvas — Orchestrator",
      "description": "Entry point for the full-screen 3D experience. Reads `gameId`/`playerId` from URL search params, gets `userId` from `useSession()` (line 20), fetches live player/game data via `useGameTeam()`, then builds a `payload` from the player's stats and starts `useMissionProcess()` polling. The Three.js `assets` and `staticGroups` are created once via `useMemo` (lines 23-24) — keeping geometry allocation outside the render loop. The `Canvas` from `@react-three/fiber` provides the WebGL context for the `<Scene>` child. Watch the `lastTerminalKeyRef` pattern (later in this file) for how mission completion is debounced — it prevents the success/failure dialog from re-firing on every poll."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/hooks/missions/useMission.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "title": "Mission Polling Loop — Stop Condition Pattern",
      "description": "`useMissionProcess` POSTs the game payload to `/missions/process-game` (which becomes `/api/backend/missions/process-game` via the BFF) and polls every 5 seconds (line 48 default) until `stopWhen()` returns true. The caller in `ThreeGameplayCanvas` stops when `data.done === true` or `data.status === 'completed'`. `refetchIntervalInBackground: true` keeps polling alive when the tab is hidden — important if the user switches away mid-game. Query key includes `userId`, `idGame`, `idPlayer` (lines 51-56) so concurrent sessions don't share cache."
    },
    {
      "title": "What You Can Now Do",
      "description": "You can now trace the full lifecycle:\n\n1. **Request arrives** → middleware checks `osb_at` cookie → redirect to `/login` if missing\n2. **Page renders** → RSC layout `await getSession()` → backend `/auth/profile` call → `SessionHydrator` seeds TanStack cache\n3. **Client mounts** → `useSession()` reads hydrated cache, no loading flash → components render with `session` populated\n4. **Data hook fires** → `api.get('/X')` → `/api/backend/X` BFF → backend with bearer from cookie → response streamed back\n5. **Auth mutation** (login/logout) → `/api/auth/X` BFF → cookies set/cleared → `qc.setQueryData(['session'], ...)`\n6. **Gameplay** → URL params → player stats fetched → mission polling loop runs → success/failure dialogs shown\n\nGood next reads: `src/lib/auth/client.ts` for the four mutation hooks; `src/components/hangar/` for the team assembly + socket scoreboard flow; `CLAUDE.md` at the project root for the conventions you should follow when adding code."
    }
  ]
}
